//! run
//! flags: -I ../../src -I support
//! stdout:
//| ok
/// The `--ast` mode of the driver gives back everything it takes. That mode
/// holds a session, a token vector and a syntax tree.
import lifetime;

//
// support/lifetime.ft states the rule of the probe, its view and the
// size of a round. Each file tests one driver mode. This keeps every program
// below the harness limit of 60 s under load.
//
// Removing each listed release moves the live view past lifetime.BOUND. The
// releases are `del(dump.data)` in driver.ast_entry, the tokens that its
// `lexer.tokvec_drop_made` gives to `del(v.items)` of lexer.tokvec_drop, the
// list of each node and `nodes` in ast.arena_drop, which `ast.arena_drop_made`
// reaches, and the three of diag.sources_drop. This test is the only run test
// that fails for a deletion of `del(dump.data)`; a deletion of the call of
// tokvec_drop_made leaves an owner unreleased, and the compiler refuses it.
// Every release of the six modes was measured this way, one deletion at a
// time, against all six tests. support/lifetime.ft gives the release count and
// the measurement. It also names the releases whose deletion moved no probe,
// and says why for each.
fn main() i32 {
    lifetime.write_runtime();
    lifetime.write_file("clean.ft", "fn main() i32 {\n    return 0;\n}\n");
    string[3] mut ast = {"fort", "--ast", "clean.ft"};
    assert(lifetime.measure(ast[0..3]) < lifetime.BOUND);
    println("ok");
    return 0;
}
